CRD 

A CRD (Custom Resource Definition) in Kubernetes allows you to create your own Kubernetes resource types.

It extends Kubernetes API beyond built-in resources like:

Pod
Deployment
Service
Ingress


Simple Understanding

Without CRD:

Kubernetes understands only built-in objects.

Example:

kind: Pod
kind: Service
kind: Deployment


With CRD:

YOU can create new resource types.

Example:

kind: Database
kind: Backup
kind: Kafka
kind: RedisCluster

These are custom resources.


Why CRDs Exist

	CRDs allow Kubernetes to manage complex applications declaratively.

Example:

	databases
	Kafka
	monitoring stacks
	cloud resources
	operators
	AI workloads



Real-World Examples

1. Prometheus Operator

	Creates custom resources:

	ServiceMonitor
	Prometheus
	AlertManager


2. ArgoCD

Creates:

	Application
	ApplicationSet


3. Cert-Manager

Creates:

	Certificate
	Issuer
	ClusterIssuer


4. Istio

	Creates:

	VirtualService
	
	Gateway

	DestinationRule
	
	CRD Architecture


CRD
  |
  |----> Defines new API resource
  |
Custom Resource (CR)
  |
  |----> Actual object instance

Example

Step 1 — Create CRD

crd.yaml


apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition

metadata:
  name: databases.mycompany.com

spec:
  group: mycompany.com

  names:
    plural: databases
    singular: database
    kind: Database
    shortNames:
      - db

  scope: Namespaced

  versions:
    - name: v1

      served: true
      storage: true

      schema:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          type: object

          properties:
            spec:
              type: object

              properties:
                engine:
                  type: string

                version:
                  type: string

                storage:
                  type: string
Apply CRD

kubectl apply -f crd.yaml

Verify CRD

kubectl get crd

Output:

databases.mycompany.com

----------------------------------------------------------


Step 2 — Create Custom Resource

database.yaml

	apiVersion: mycompany.com/v1
	kind: Database

	metadata:
	  name: postgres-db

	spec:
	  engine: postgres
	  version: "16"
	  storage: 20Gi

Apply Resource

	kubectl apply -f database.yaml

View Custom Resource

	kubectl get databases

or:

	kubectl get db

Example:

	NAME          	AGE
	postgres-db   	1m

------------------------------

Describe Resource

kubectl describe db postgres-db


What Happens Internally

CRD only creates API structure.


By itself:

CRD does NOTHING

Usually an Operator watches CRDs.

CRD + Operator

This is the real power.



Example:

Database CR created
      |
Operator watches it
      |
Creates:
  - StatefulSet
  - PVC
  - Service
  - ConfigMap

Automatically.


What Is an Operator?

Operator = Controller + CRD

It automates application management.

Famous 					Operators

Operator					Purpose
Prometheus 					Operator	Monitoring
Elastic Operator			Elasticsearch
Strimzi						Kafka
ArgoCD						GitOps
Cert Manager				TLS certificates

Check Existing CRDs

Run:

kubectl get crd

You may see:

applications.argoproj.io
certificates.cert-manager.io
servicemonitors.monitoring.coreos.com


Get CRD Details

	kubectl describe crd databases.mycompany.com

Delete CRD

	kubectl delete crd databases.mycompany.com

WARNING:
This also deletes all associated custom resources.